WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:05.660
The spookiest time of year is quickly approaching,
and if you're looking for a gory movie marathon,

00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:08.940
you could do a lot worse than the Friday the
13th series.

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:13.210
Friday may not be the father of the slasher
genre, but it's definitely the crazy uncle

00:00:13.210 --> 00:00:17.930
— and the unstoppable Jason Voorhees' signature
hockey mask is now one of the most recognizable

00:00:17.930 --> 00:00:19.980
icons in American pop culture.

00:00:20.130 --> 00:00:25.060
For newcomers and hardcore fans alike, we're
running down every Friday the 13th movie,

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:26.540
ranked from worst to best.

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:32.820
After sprinting past the events of the original
film, the 2009 reboot moves on to an adult

00:00:32.830 --> 00:00:37.560
Jason with an intelligent, cunning characterization
that seemed like a pointless change, if not

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:39.940
an outright betrayal of the original series.

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:44.920
Jason's seemingly supernatural abilities are
neatly explained away, callbacks to the previous

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:49.800
films fall ridiculously flat, and Jason captures
a girl and holds her prisoner — as if he'd

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:51.440
ever bother with mere kidnapping.

00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:56.500
The film had a so-so performance at the box
office but earned dismal reviews, and a lengthy

00:00:56.500 --> 00:01:00.400
tug of war over rights issues helped sink
another attempt at a reboot.

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:05.560
This film simply shouldn't exist — even
the worst entry from the original series is

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:06.060
preferable.

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:07.060
On that note…

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:11.659
Just one in a long line of films that were
meant to end the series, Jason Goes to Hell:

00:01:11.659 --> 00:01:16.460
The Final Friday undoes the mythology built
by the previous entries, reinventing Jason

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:21.100
as a vengeful spirit that can only be taken
by members of his own bloodline using a special

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:21.980
magic dagger.

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:27.020
After being taken by the FBI in the beginning,
Jason spends most of the movie as a spirit,

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:31.439
possessing random people by forcibly installing
his heart into their chest cavities.

00:01:31.439 --> 00:01:35.639
Its only notable moment comes at the very
end, when an appearance by a familiar knife-gloved

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:39.580
hand sets up a much better movie — that
wouldn't show up for another decade.

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:46.320
A film that definitely fails to live up to
its title, 1989's Friday the 13th Part VIII:

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:50.369
Jason Takes Manhattan benefits only from the
presence of Kane Hodder in his second turn

00:01:50.369 --> 00:01:51.700
as the man behind the mask.

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:56.020
A ridiculous amount of the movie takes place
in the confines of a cruise ship, while Jason's

00:01:56.020 --> 00:02:00.620
actual romp through the Big Apple takes up
perhaps 20 minutes — at the movie's end.

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:03.780
"You don't understand, someone is trying to
kill us!"

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:07.080
"Welcome to New York."

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:10.940
Of course, director Rob Hedden had intended
something very different.

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:15.320
As he explained in a making-of book, Jason
was supposed to slay his way through all sorts

00:02:15.330 --> 00:02:20.370
of iconic New York locations before jumping
off the Statue of Liberty, but the measly

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:22.600
$5 million budget wouldn't allow for any of
that.

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:27.000
Instead of that awesome-sounding movie, we
got one of the most visually flat, tedious

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:28.880
entries in the entire series.

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:33.480
Every long-running horror franchise eventually
embarrasses itself by sending its villain

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:34.220
into space.

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:39.120
So it's only fitting that the final proper
installment of the original Friday the 13th

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:40.240
series would do just that.

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:44.940
After being captured by the government, Jason
is placed into cryogenic stasis for hundreds

00:02:44.950 --> 00:02:45.950
of years.

00:02:45.950 --> 00:02:50.391
Revived by some students, he wreaks his special
brand of havoc on a spaceship, receives an

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:54.260
insane cyborg upgrade, and is once again seemingly
dispatched for good.

00:02:54.400 --> 00:03:00.020
Despite its silly premise, 2001's Jason X
is surprisingly solid, with a ferocious performance

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:04.569
by Hodder in his final outing and at least
a couple of the series' most truly bizarre

00:03:04.569 --> 00:03:05.840
and inventive methods.

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:10.680
A late-film sequence featuring cyborg Jason
in a Holodeck-like recreation of Camp Crystal

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:14.849
Lake is also a nice touch, and features a
remixed version of what Hodder called his

00:03:14.849 --> 00:03:18.849
favorite slay of all time, which just happens
to be featured in the next entry.

00:03:19.859 --> 00:03:25.489
Despite being little-remembered, 1988's Friday
the 13th Part VII: The New Blood is noteworthy

00:03:25.489 --> 00:03:30.300
for marking Kane Hodder's debut as Jason,
and also for the infamous sleeping bag scene.

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:37.900
The film's protagonist is Tina Shepard, a
telekinetic young woman who inadvertently

00:03:37.909 --> 00:03:42.239
revives Jason from his watery grave at the
bottom of Crystal Lake while trying to bring

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:43.920
her drowned father back to life.

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:48.000
Many fans felt that the "Jason vs. Carrie"
premise was a bit of a stretch.

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:51.840
But Hodder held the film together, and he
even helped the film set a record for stunt

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:52.340
work.

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:57.280
In the film's climactic battle, Tina uses
her powers to set Jason on fire, a scene that

00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:02.300
was achieved by having Hodder actually be
on fire for a whopping 40 seconds as the cameras

00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:02.800
rolled.

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:07.240
His dedication to the role showed early, and
his hot performance helps make The New Blood

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:08.280
a solid entry.

00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:13.240
It wasn't until 2003 that fans' hopes and
the promise made in the last few frames of

00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:15.700
Jason Goes to Hell were finally fulfilled.

00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:20.040
Fortunately, the movie of fans' nightmares
ended up being worth the years-long wait.

00:04:20.250 --> 00:04:25.440
While it isn't actually part of the main Friday
the 13th franchise, the crossover Freddy vs.

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:29.420
Jason earns its place on this list by virtue
of being so awesomely over the top.

00:04:38.110 --> 00:04:41.720
A Nightmare On Elm Street's Freddy Krueger
is bummed that his memory among the living

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:45.200
is dying out, making it harder for him to
invade the dreams of children.

00:04:45.510 --> 00:04:49.920
He decides to free Jason Voorhees from Hell
and turn him loose on his old Elm Street stomping

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:54.220
grounds in the hopes that the fresh wave of
gruesome happenings will rekindle the legend

00:04:54.220 --> 00:04:54.880
of Freddy.

00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:59.380
But once Jason starts slaying, it's pretty
tough to get him to stop, so Freddy maneuvers

00:04:59.380 --> 00:05:03.660
Jason into a showdown before he can slay off
all of Freddy's potential victims.

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:08.080
The film works far better than it has any
right to after spending an eternity in development

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:08.580
hell.

00:05:08.580 --> 00:05:12.920
The franchises' styles mesh well and there
are a couple of genuinely creepy moments amidst

00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:13.620
the carnage.

00:05:14.620 --> 00:05:19.960
The fifth film in the series, 1985's Friday
the 13th: A New Beginning is a near-perfect

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:25.140
Friday the 13th film...that fans absolutely
hate for one totally understandable reason.

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:28.720
The ending of the movie reveals that the Jason
who's been slaying his way through a teen

00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:34.500
rehab facility wasn't ever actually Jason
at all, but just some...random guy — specifically

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:37.880
a paramedic, upset over the passing of his
son early in the film.

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:38.980
Okay.

00:05:39.340 --> 00:05:43.860
The Jason copycat's weak motivation was especially
disappointing considering the interesting

00:05:43.870 --> 00:05:47.160
psychological thriller angle the film takes
before the reveal.

00:05:47.160 --> 00:05:51.650
A now teenaged Tommy Jarvis, who as a child
put a brutal end to Jason's reign of terror

00:05:51.650 --> 00:05:55.990
with the slayer's own machete, is among the
residents at a home for troubled teens when

00:05:55.990 --> 00:05:57.100
the happenings begin anew.

00:05:57.200 --> 00:06:02.180
The implication that Tommy may be responsible
for this new round of mayhem is teased throughout,

00:06:02.180 --> 00:06:05.760
and his ultimate face-off with the Jason copycat
is truly nerve-jangling.

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:10.300
But for most fans, the fakeout ending wasted
all the good will the movie engendered with

00:06:10.300 --> 00:06:12.260
its crazy ways and high body count.

00:06:12.380 --> 00:06:17.280
It all makes A New Beginning a very good franchise
entry with one king-sized flaw that ultimately

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:19.240
shouldn't keep it from being enjoyed.

00:06:19.900 --> 00:06:25.460
The third entry in the Friday the 13th franchise
had the misfortune of being produced in 1982,

00:06:25.460 --> 00:06:28.380
smack in the middle of the early '80s 3-D
revival.

00:06:28.440 --> 00:06:32.820
But if you can get past all the unnecessary
3-D shots that only exist to throw something

00:06:32.830 --> 00:06:37.830
at the audience, Part III is a tight, suspenseful
shocker that features some jaw-dropping things

00:06:37.830 --> 00:06:41.180
and sees Jason put on his iconic hockey mask
for the first time.

00:06:41.390 --> 00:06:45.140
The film's plot is nothing special: Jason
removes a bunch of teenage friends and their

00:06:45.140 --> 00:06:46.400
biker gang rivals.

00:06:46.440 --> 00:06:51.180
Producers toyed with the idea of definitively
ending the franchise with this entry, scripting

00:06:51.180 --> 00:06:55.020
a conclusion in which Jason's head is lopped
off by protagonist Chris.

00:06:55.020 --> 00:06:59.650
But ultimately, Jason's fate is left hanging
after a nightmarish false ending that illustrates

00:06:59.650 --> 00:07:01.950
how fakeout dream sequences should be done.

00:07:02.950 --> 00:07:09.670
At a scant 87 minutes, 1981's Friday the 13th
Part 2 is short, dark, and efficient.

00:07:09.670 --> 00:07:12.330
When the carnage starts, it comes fast and
furious.

00:07:12.330 --> 00:07:17.620
The movie wastes no time unceremoniously dispatching
Alice, the sole survivor of the first film,

00:07:17.620 --> 00:07:18.880
within its first few minutes.

00:07:19.040 --> 00:07:21.340
This installment's Jason is a mute, ferocious
beast.

00:07:21.380 --> 00:07:26.090
And the means by which he's defeated — confusing
him by invoking the memory of his dead mother

00:07:26.090 --> 00:07:30.260
— would become a reliable means of at least
slowing him down in future entries in the

00:07:30.260 --> 00:07:30.760
series.

00:07:30.780 --> 00:07:35.340
Despite the departure of the first film's
special effects wizard Tom Savini, Part 2

00:07:35.340 --> 00:07:40.070
offered passing sequences creative enough
to invoke the wrath of the MPAA, who insisted

00:07:40.070 --> 00:07:44.110
that a total of 48 seconds be cut to avoid
an X rating.

00:07:44.110 --> 00:07:48.500
It's still a brutal, relentlessly paced entry
and a worthy sequel to one of the greatest

00:07:48.500 --> 00:07:49.920
slasher films of all time.

00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:54.980
The film that launched a seemingly unstoppable
franchise is actually more of a crazy mystery

00:07:54.980 --> 00:07:57.040
than most of the slasher flicks it inspired.

00:07:57.270 --> 00:08:01.660
The third act reveal that the guy is former
camp cook Mrs. Voorhees, driven insane by

00:08:01.670 --> 00:08:06.281
the drowning passing of her weirdo son Jason,
was absolutely jaw-dropping — as was her

00:08:06.281 --> 00:08:09.860
ultimate fate, thanks to the film's special
effects genius, Tom Savini.

00:08:09.860 --> 00:08:14.210
According to director Sean Cunningham, Savini
obsessed endlessly over how to accomplish

00:08:14.210 --> 00:08:18.990
Mrs. Voorhees' final scene, and he also designed
young Jason's look for his surprise appearance

00:08:18.990 --> 00:08:19.480
at the end.

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:25.000
Friday the 13th was a film born of pure money-making
opportunism, but Savini's groundbreaking makeup

00:08:25.000 --> 00:08:27.300
effects work turned it into a horror classic.

00:08:27.550 --> 00:08:32.800
Of course, it also doesn't hurt that the performance
of veteran actress Betsy Palmer as Mrs. Voorhees

00:08:32.800 --> 00:08:34.180
is super creepy.

00:08:34.540 --> 00:08:35.360
"Slay her, mommy.

00:08:36.120 --> 00:08:36.980
slay her."

00:08:37.740 --> 00:08:42.200
The second-best film in the franchise has
it all: tons of campy takings, skin-crawling

00:08:42.210 --> 00:08:44.650
scares, and great performances all around.

00:08:44.650 --> 00:08:49.260
The film opens with a delirious sequence in
which Tommy Jarvis literally digs up Jason's

00:08:49.260 --> 00:08:53.960
grave on a stormy night, impales the corpse
with an iron stake from the cemetery's fence,

00:08:53.960 --> 00:08:57.440
and angrily tosses a hockey mask and machete
into the grave.

00:08:57.720 --> 00:08:59.260
"F--- you, Jason."

00:08:59.980 --> 00:09:04.380
Anybody who's ever seen Frankenstein can guess
what happens next: there's a freak lightning

00:09:04.390 --> 00:09:09.140
strike, and a newly reanimated Jason — an
explicitly supernatural being for the first

00:09:09.140 --> 00:09:11.520
time in the series — goes on a slaying spree.

00:09:16.520 --> 00:09:21.050
The opening sets a self-referential tone that
continues throughout the movie, successfully

00:09:21.050 --> 00:09:24.650
making audiences laugh between the jump scares
and brutal happenings.

00:09:24.650 --> 00:09:29.330
The film is simply a blast: the body count
is absurdly high, and stuntman C.J. Graham

00:09:29.330 --> 00:09:33.180
is second only to Kane Hodder in his menacing
portrayal of Jason.

00:09:33.180 --> 00:09:35.360
It's almost the best Friday the 13th ever.

00:09:35.660 --> 00:09:36.360
Almost.

00:09:37.140 --> 00:09:41.390
As the name suggests, the fourth installment
of the series was indeed intended to be the

00:09:41.390 --> 00:09:42.100
final one.

00:09:42.390 --> 00:09:45.820
And if it had been, the franchise may have
had a completely different legacy.

00:09:45.830 --> 00:09:50.260
The film begins with Jason escaping the morgue
and getting set to embark on another rampage

00:09:50.260 --> 00:09:53.500
— before finally meeting his match in an
unlikely adversary.

00:09:53.680 --> 00:09:58.440
Jason's soon-to-be long-time nemesis Tommy
Jarvis makes his first appearance as a 12-year

00:09:58.440 --> 00:10:00.500
old, played by a young Corey Feldman.

00:10:00.890 --> 00:10:04.820
Tommy ends up saving his sister's life in
the film's nerve-wracking climax, one of the

00:10:04.820 --> 00:10:08.600
most intense and graphic final scenes in any
mainstream horror film.

00:10:08.820 --> 00:10:13.060
Ask any truly hardcore fan, and they'll tell
you: The Final Chapter is the quintessential

00:10:13.060 --> 00:10:14.540
Friday the 13th film.

00:10:14.890 --> 00:10:19.680
Unbelievably brutal, and at times unbearably
tense, it's not only the best film in the

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:22.600
franchise, but one of the greatest slasher
films ever.

